<h1>Orcraft Test Batch</h1>
<p>A batch sits above suites. It holds any mix of suites, groups and tests, runs them as one and
  summarizes them as one. It is what a CI snapshot exported from the controller puts on top of its
  mirrors, and what you write yourself to run several suites, possibly from several repositories, with
  a single command. Like a suite it is declared rather than coded.</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from __future__ import annotations

from orcraft import (
	OrcraftBatchItem,
	OrcraftConfigField,
	OrcraftTestBatch,
	OrcraftTestBatchConfig,
	OrcraftTestBatchResult,
)

from my_suite import MySuite
from smoke.smoke_group import SmokeGroup


class NightlyBatchConfig(OrcraftTestBatchConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="Nightly")
	description = OrcraftConfigField.Text(
		default="Everything that must be green before a release."
	)
	items = OrcraftConfigField.Items(
		default_factory=lambda: [
			MySuite(),
			SmokeGroup(),
			OrcraftBatchItem(
				name="Test Suite: Network",
				kind="suite",
				source_path="../orcraft-test-suite-network/test_suite_network.py",
				class_name="TestSuiteNetwork",
				import_roots=("../orcraft-test-suite-network",),
			),
		]
	)


class NightlyBatchResult(OrcraftTestBatchResult):
	pass


class NightlyBatch(OrcraftTestBatch):
	def __init__(self) -> None:
		super().__init__(config=NightlyBatchConfig())
		self.result = NightlyBatchResult()


def main() -> None:
	NightlyBatch().run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
	main()') }}

<h2 id="items-field">Items field</h2>
<p><code>items</code> uses <code>default_factory</code> for the same reason
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/suites/#groups-field"><code>groups</code></a> does. An item is
  one of two things:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Item</th>
      <th>What it is</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>An instance</td>
      <td>A suite, group or test object, imported and constructed in the batch file like a group's tests.
        Its repository is on the batch file's import path already.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>OrcraftBatchItem</code></td>
      <td>A reference to a suite, group or test by source file and class name. The class is imported
        only inside the subprocess that runs it, with the roots listed in <code>import_roots</code> put
        first on that subprocess's import path.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>References exist for one reason: two repositories may both define a top-level package with the same
  name - a <code>common</code> package, say - and importing both suites into one interpreter would let
  one shadow the other. A reference keeps each item in its own interpreter all the way down, which is
  why a CI snapshot lists its items this way.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th><code>OrcraftBatchItem</code> field</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>source_path</code></td>
      <td>The <code>.py</code> file that defines the class.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>class_name</code></td>
      <td>The class to instantiate from that file.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>name</code></td>
      <td>How the item is listed before it runs - normally the component's own name, such as
        <code>"Test Suite: Network"</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>kind</code></td>
      <td><code>"suite"</code>, <code>"group"</code> or <code>"test"</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>import_roots</code></td>
      <td>Directories put first on the subprocess's import path, in this order. A CI mirror lists the
        snapshot folder and the live suite repository.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>package_source_path</code></td>
      <td>The repository whose code endpoint uploads ship, when it is not the folder the class was loaded
        from. A CI mirror points it at the live repository it mirrors.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>label</code></td>
      <td>A display label, kept so the controller can show it again when it opens the batch.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Relative paths in a reference are relative to the batch file, so a folder holding a batch and its
  mirrors can be copied as a whole.</p>

<h2 id="config-fields">Config fields</h2>
<h3 id="config-code">Overridable in code</h3>
<p>Set in your config class, fixed for the run. A batch has no run configuration panel: the controller
  shows a batch's items, never the batch itself.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Default</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>name</code></td>
      <td><code>"Test Batch"</code></td>
      <td>Display name in the CLI and logs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>description</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>Free text describing what the batch covers.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>version</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>Batch version, recorded with the results.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>items</code></td>
      <td><code>[]</code></td>
      <td>The suites, groups, tests and references this batch runs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_orientation</code></td>
      <td><code>HORIZONTAL</code></td>
      <td>Orientation of the logged config table, or <code>OFF</code> to leave it out of the log.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warning">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Override carefully</span>
  <p>Redeclare a field with the same <code>OrcraftConfigField</code> type and a new default -
    <code>name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="Nightly")</code>. A bare assignment such as
    <code>name = "Nightly"</code> is not an override: it shadows the field with a plain attribute, and the
    log table no longer sees it.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="config-reserved">Reserved</h3>
<p>Written by the framework while a run is in progress.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>start_time</code></td>
      <td>When the run started. Stamped by the framework at run time - leave it alone.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_tab</code></td>
      <td>Log indentation. Assigned by the framework at every level - it cannot be overridden, only collided with.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_title_color</code>, <code>log_title_bg_color</code></td>
      <td>Palette plumbing for the log banners. Not user-facing.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--caution">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Do not redeclare</span>
  <p>A field of your own under one of these names replaces the framework's. The framework keeps writing
    to the name, but through your definition - and the log indentation and the run timestamps silently
    stop meaning what the controller expects.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="execution">Execution</h2>
<p>A batch always runs its items concurrently, each in its own subprocess. Suite and group items start at
  once and start their own children; the tests of the whole batch, test items included, take their turn
  in one queue kept for the run, in the order the items list them, and a test starts when every endpoint
  it needs is free of the batch's other tests, exactly as it would under the controller. When an item
  finishes, its whole log is appended to the batch log as one block, so the batch log reads as nested
  blocks: the batch header, then each item's complete log as it finishes, then the batch summary.</p>

<p>A batch cannot contain another batch.</p>

<h2 id="results">Result fields</h2>
<p>A batch has no body: its result is written entirely by the framework as its items complete, and an
  empty <code>OrcraftTestBatchResult</code> subclass reports everything below. Items may be of different
  kinds, so the batch counts what every kind has, tests and cases, plus the items themselves.</p>

<p class="docs-legend"><span class="mark mark--user">{% icon "check" %}</span> overridable by user (optional)
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <span class="mark mark--framework">{% icon "cpu" %}</span> written by the framework, may still be set directly
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <span class="mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</span> reserved</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Inherited field</th>
      <th class="mark-head">Overridden by user (optional)</th>
      <th class="mark-head">Reserved</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>status</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td><code class="status-pass">PASS</code>, <code class="status-fail">FAIL</code> or <code class="status-error">ERROR</code>, derived from the items.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-fail">failure_description</code>, <code class="status-error">error_description</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_description</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>The first such description reported by an item.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>finish_time</code>, <code>elapsed_time</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>When the batch finished and how long it took.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_orientation</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--user">{% icon "check" %}</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td>Whether the result table is logged horizontally, vertically or not at all (<code>OFF</code>). Defaults to vertical.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_title_color</code>, <code>log_title_bg_color</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>Palette plumbing for the log banners. Not user-facing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>total_items</code>, <code class="status-pass">passed_items</code>, <code class="status-fail">failed_items</code>, <code class="status-error">error_items</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_items</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>Item counters, one per suite, group or test in the batch.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>total_tests</code>, <code class="status-pass">passed_tests</code>, <code class="status-fail">failed_tests</code>, <code class="status-error">error_tests</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_tests</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>Test counters, summed across every item; a test item counts as one test.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>total_cases</code>, <code class="status-pass">passed_cases</code>, <code class="status-fail">failed_cases</code>, <code class="status-error">error_cases</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_cases</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>Case counters, summed across every test of every item.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warning">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Override carefully</span>
  <p>The only field that is yours here is <code>log_orientation</code>, and only its default: redeclare it
    with the same <code>OrcraftResultField</code> type -
    <code>log_orientation = OrcraftResultField.Format(default=OrcraftResultField.LogTableOrientation.VERTICAL)</code>
    - never as a bare value, which shadows the field so the log table no longer sees it.</p>
</div>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--caution">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Do not redeclare</span>
  <p>A field of your own under any of these names replaces the framework's. The framework keeps writing
    to the name, but through your definition - and status comparisons, the three levels of counters and
    the Items table silently break. Only <code>log_orientation</code> is meant to be overridden, and only
    to change its default.</p>
</div>

<p>After its result table the batch logs an <em>Items</em> table, one row per item with that item's status,
  descriptions, times and counters, and then its warnings, failures and errors tables with an
  <em>Item</em> column in front, so a failing case can be traced to the item, group and test it belongs
  to.</p>

<h2 id="running">Running</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Where</th>
      <th>How</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Standalone</td>
      <td>Execute the batch file directly, via its <code>main()</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Controller GUI</td>
      <td>Add the batch file like any other item. The controller flattens it: its items land at the top of
        the session tree and the batch itself never becomes a node. An item written by the CI export shows
        the original suite, group or test with the exported values as its plan and the CI tag on each test.
        The session itself is untouched.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>CI</td>
      <td>A CI snapshot exported from the controller is a batch: <code>batch.py</code> lists the exported
        items as references to the mirrors beside it, and <code>ci_run.py</code> runs it on a CI machine,
        resolving each item's repository from <code>--suites-root</code>, from an
        <code>ORCRAFT_SUITE_ROOT_&lt;REPO&gt;</code> variable or from the paths baked in at export time,
        and exiting with <code>0</code>, <code>10</code> or <code>20</code> for pass, fail or error.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
